WEBVTT

1
00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:20.133
For days Jo locked herself up in her sanctuary, telling everyone that she was working on a new novel, when in fact she was bawling her eyes out.

2
00:00:20.134 --> 00:00:27.556
She didn't fool anybody, because it was clear from her puffy red eyes and swollen nose that she hadn't spent the night writing.

3
00:00:27.557 --> 00:00:35.767
Having decided it was time to face reality, Mother gently knocked on the door, and said, "Jo? May I come in?"

4
00:00:35.768 --> 00:00:45.429
"Mmm . . .," Jo said in a muffled voice. Seeing Mother's kind, understanding eyes, Jo broke out in a new wave of tears.

5
00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:51.213
"Shh, darling, just let it out," comforted Mother.

6
00:00:51.214 --> 00:00:58.028
"It's not fair," wept Jo. "She knew how much I wanted to travel to Europe.

7
00:00:58.029 --> 00:01:07.661
Every year she would make me believe that the next year I could come along, but now to choose Amy instead . . . it's unbearable."

8
00:01:07.662 --> 00:01:14.070
"Do you remember how you used to feel when you came home each day from Aunt March's?" asked Mother.

9
00:01:14.071 --> 00:01:20.753
Sniffing, Jo replied, "Yes. She annoyed me to no end."

10
00:01:20.754 --> 00:01:26.500
"Now imagine if you had to spend all day and night with her for one year.

11
00:01:26.501 --> 00:01:31.104
Honestly, Jo, do you think you could manage that?" Mother reasoned with her.

12
00:01:31.105 --> 00:01:38.294
Wiping her nose with the back of her hand, Jo thought about it but did not answer, so Mother continued,

13
00:01:38.295 --> 00:01:43.199
"You once told me about the hundreds of libraries and museums you wanted to visit.

14
00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:47.495
Do you think Aunt March would allow you the freedom to do that?"

15
00:01:47.496 --> 00:01:53.072
Jo, who had stopped crying, shook her head and said, "I never thought about it that way.

16
00:01:53.073 --> 00:01:58.823
You're right, Mother, Aunt March would never let me follow my own personal dreams.

17
00:01:58.824 --> 00:02:02.997
Instead I would be at her beck and call every moment of the day."

18
00:02:02.998 --> 00:02:09.352
Patting Jo on the back, Mother said, "But Amy reacts differently as Aunt March rightly sees.

19
00:02:09.353 --> 00:02:14.660
Amy is quite happy going for tea, socializing, and taking strolls.

20
00:02:14.661 --> 00:02:21.195
That's why she would be a better companion on this particular trip. Do you understand that now?"

21
00:02:21.196 --> 00:02:24.539
Jo nodded heartily and said, "Yes, Mother.

22
00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:29.952
I'm still jealous that Amy is going to see Europe before me, but she deserves it.

23
00:02:29.953 --> 00:02:33.905
She is very good to Aunt March and to our neighbors."

24
00:02:33.906 --> 00:02:38.376
Jo told Mother how well behaved Amy had been when they made their calls.

25
00:02:38.377 --> 00:02:44.165
Remembering how hard her sister worked to please others reminded Jo of Amy's words on the road:

26
00:02:44.166 --> 00:02:50.843
"I still believe that good will come if we try to show kindness to others regardless of how they see us."

27
00:02:50.844 --> 00:02:56.568
Jo had to admit that she learned something quite valuable from her sister through this incident.

28
00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:02.016
In the girls' bedroom, Amy had a lot to do in preparation for her trip.

29
00:03:02.017 --> 00:03:06.577
"Aunt March says not to pack too many things since she will give me an allowance

30
00:03:06.578 --> 00:03:12.112
so I can get my dresses tailored in the European style," Amy said excitedly to Beth.

31
00:03:12.113 --> 00:03:19.166
"Europeans are so cultured and stylish. I think you're going to fit right in, Amy," said Beth.

32
00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:27.492
"Thank you, Beth. You are too kind and I'm going to miss you terribly. I really am," Amy said, hugging her tightly.

33
00:03:27.493 --> 00:03:32.456
"Have you packed away Mr. Huggie?" Jo called out loudly as she walked in.

34
00:03:32.457 --> 00:03:37.687
Mr. Huggie was an old stuffed animal that Amy used to sleep with when she was a little girl.

35
00:03:37.688 --> 00:03:44.585
"Unfortunately there is no room for him in my suitcase," Amy said, pointing to her oversized luggage.

36
00:03:44.586 --> 00:03:48.107
"But he's going to miss you," Jo teased.

37
00:03:48.108 --> 00:03:52.493
"I feel better knowing he's here with you," Amy said in return.

38
00:03:52.494 --> 00:03:58.874
After Aunt March announced that she was taking Amy with her, it put a strain on Jo and Amy's relationship.

39
00:03:58.875 --> 00:04:05.853
Jo wasn't angry, but under the circumstances, she found it hard, as anyone would, not to be resentful.

40
00:04:05.854 --> 00:04:10.811
Now, however, it appeared that the tension had lifted and life was back to normal.

41
00:04:10.812 --> 00:04:16.867
"You deserve to go, Amy, and I hope you have the time of your life," Jo said sincerely.

42
00:04:16.868 --> 00:04:19.566
"Do you mean that, Jo?" asked Amy.

43
00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:25.805
"Yes, and I'm sorry for behaving immaturely," begged Jo. "Will you forgive me?"

44
00:04:25.806 --> 00:04:30.656
Amy hugged her sister hard and cried, "Oh, Jo, there's nothing to forgive.

45
00:04:30.657 --> 00:04:33.440
I'm just relieved that you aren't upset at me.

46
00:04:33.441 --> 00:04:39.107
I'll write as often as I can and tell you all about the places I see and the people I meet."

47
00:04:39.108 --> 00:04:43.101
"I'd like that, Amy. I really would," said Jo.

48
00:04:43.102 --> 00:04:48.010
Having accepted that she might never have a chance to visit Europe in her lifetime,

49
00:04:48.011 --> 00:04:54.588
Jo was busying her days with writing, but she could not shake a restlessness that had developed in her heart.

50
00:04:54.589 --> 00:05:01.307
Lying in the yard one afternoon, Jo looked up at the sky and saw a couple of birds swirling around.

51
00:05:01.308 --> 00:05:07.419
"I wish I could spread my wings and fly off like you," she said to them dreamily.

52
00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:12.672
The dark shadow of a human face blocked her view just then, disrupting her daydream.

53
00:05:12.673 --> 00:05:20.921
"If you had wings, how would anybody be able to catch you?" the face said, showing that he had clearly been listening to her private monologue.

54
00:05:20.922 --> 00:05:27.411
"The whole point is never to be caught but to have the freedom to go wherever you choose," Jo replied.

55
00:05:27.412 --> 00:05:31.385
"And how rude of you to sneak up on an unsuspecting person."

56
00:05:31.386 --> 00:05:37.497
Reaching out to the hand extended toward her, Jo sat up and grinned into the face of her best friend.

57
00:05:37.498 --> 00:05:41.371
"What are you doing home at this time of the week?" she asked Laurie.

58
00:05:41.372 --> 00:05:43.899
He shrugged and changed the subject instead.

59
00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:46.894
"I hear Amy's off to Europe with your old aunt."

60
00:05:46.895 --> 00:05:51.067
"That's right. She's going for one year. Now back to you.

61
00:05:51.068 --> 00:05:56.057
Why are you home?" Jo smartly brought the conversation back to Laurie.

62
00:05:56.058 --> 00:05:59.412
"I've been suspended," Laurie finally admitted.

63
00:05:59.413 --> 00:06:04.520
"Why? Laurie, what happened?" Jo asked in an alarmed voice.

64
00:06:04.521 --> 00:06:09.830
"I got in a fight with these guys and well . . . here I am," he said lightly.

65
00:06:09.831 --> 00:06:12.935
"How long are you suspended for?" Jo asked.

66
00:06:12.936 --> 00:06:15.687
"Maybe two weeks," Laurie replied.

67
00:06:15.688 --> 00:06:23.968
Jo gazed at him with concern knowing that he was going to college to please his grandfather, but he hadn't quite adapted to the life there.

68
00:06:23.969 --> 00:06:27.649
She wanted to scold him but something stopped her.

69
00:06:27.650 --> 00:06:35.265
Instead she said, "Well, I'm glad you're here because after Amy leaves tomorrow, I'm going to be so miserable.

70
00:06:35.266 --> 00:06:38.252
You can be a good boy and get me through this."

71
00:06:38.253 --> 00:06:46.621
Grinning mischievously, Laurie saluted her and said, "Yes, sir! I live to carry out your orders."

72
00:06:46.622 --> 00:06:55.380
The next day bright and early, after Amy managed to fit all her suitcases, paint sets, easel, and other art supplies into Aunt March's carriage,

73
00:06:55.381 --> 00:06:57.691
she said her farewells to her family.

74
00:06:57.692 --> 00:07:04.294
"Good-bye, everyone! I'll miss you all very much," she cried out, giving each person a hug.

75
00:07:04.295 --> 00:07:08.500
"Be careful not to catch a cold," Mother said tearfully.

76
00:07:08.501 --> 00:07:11.884
"Remember to write often," said her father.

77
00:07:11.885 --> 00:07:17.183
"Find yourself a handsome Italian prince," were Jo's parting words.

78
00:07:17.184 --> 00:07:22.099
"Please think of us because we'll be thinking of you," pleaded Beth.

79
00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:25.232
"Take care of yourself," cried Meg and John.

80
00:07:25.233 --> 00:07:29.031
"I may make a surprise visit on you," Laurie called out.

81
00:07:29.032 --> 00:07:32.714
"That would be wonderful, Laurie. I do hope you'll come.

82
00:07:32.715 --> 00:07:41.652
Bye! Good-bye!" Amy shouted, as the carriage disappeared around the corner.
